      en: "Agua Blanca is an archaeological site and community in Manabí province, Ecuador, known for the pre-Columbian Manteño culture. The site lies within Machalilla National Park and contains remains of a settlement occupied between 500 and 1500 AD. Excavations have revealed houses, ceremonial areas, and a complex irrigation system. The local community runs a small museum displaying pottery, shell work, and stone sculptures. Agua Blanca is also an example of sustainable tourism, where residents preserve their cultural heritage. The site is surrounded by dry forest and offers insights into the life of the Manteño, who were known for seafaring and trade in Spondylus shells.",
